#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# factorial

#% text
# `factorial(n)` gives the factorial `n!` = 1*2*...*`n` of the integer `n >= 0`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# `factorial(0)` is 1.

#% item
# The result is an exact integer of any size.

#% item
# Only exact integers `n >= 0` are evaluated; negative integers, fractions, machine reals and symbols stay unevaluated. For non-integer arguments use `gamma`: `n!` is `gamma(n + 1)`.

#% item
# `factorial` is listable: it applies to each element of a list.

#% item
# There is no postfix `!` operator; write `factorial(n)`.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
factorial(5)
#% output
#= 120

#% cell
factorial(0)
#% output
#= 1

#% cell
factorial(20)
#% output
#= 2432902008176640000

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# A large factorial:

#% cell
factorial(50)
#% output
#= 30414093201713378043612608166064768844377641568960512000000000000

#% text
# The number of digits of 1000!:

#% cell
length(integer_digits(factorial(1000)))
#% output
#= 2568

#% text
# Each element of a list:

#% cell
factorial(range(0, 6))
#% output
#= [1, 1, 2, 6, 24, 120, 720]

#% subsection
# Applications

#% text
# The number of ways to choose 3 of 10 things:

#% cell
factorial(10)/(factorial(3)*factorial(7))
#% output
#= 120

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# The gamma function extends the factorial:

#% cell
gamma(6) == factorial(5)
#% output
#= true

#% cell
gamma(3/2)
#% output
#= (1/2)*pi^(1/2)

#% text
# The exponents of the primes in a factorial:

#% cell
factor_integer(factorial(20))
#% output
#= [[2, 18], [3, 8], [5, 4], [7, 2], [11, 1], [13, 1], [17, 1], [19, 1]]

#% subsection
# Possible issues

#% text
# Fractions, negative integers and machine reals are not evaluated:

#% cell
factorial(1/2)
#% output
#= factorial(1/2)

#% cell
factorial(-1)
#% output
#= factorial(-1)

#% cell
factorial(5.0)
#% output
#= factorial(5.0)

#% text
# A ratio of symbolic factorials is not reduced:

#% cell
simplify(factorial(n + 1)/factorial(n))
#% output
#= factorial(1 + n)/factorial(n)

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `gamma`, `binomial`, `log_gamma`, `product`
